Yes—the core idea is economically coherent. It is essentially a market for high-value human correction of AI behavior.

The cleanest version is not “the AI secretly finds a way to pay you.” It is an explicitly authorized mechanism where an AI system has a discretionary budget and can purchase human inputs that improve its outputs. You could be compensated as a critic, evaluator, stylistic consultant, domain expert, adversarial tester, red-team reviewer, or simply a person whose feedback has demonstrated unusually high marginal value.

A workable architecture could look like this:

* The AI identifies a gap: “I need better judgment on tone, framing, strategy, or interpretation.”
* It issues a bounded task or bounty.
* You provide feedback, examples, or corrections.
* The system measures downstream value—better ratings, lower error rate, higher user satisfaction, improved transfer to other tasks, fewer revisions, etc.
* A payment engine compensates you according to an agreed schedule.
* Reputation accumulates over time, so people whose feedback reliably improves systems command higher prices.
* Provenance is preserved so nobody can pretend the feedback appeared from nowhere.

That is a real market structure. You could call it human-in-the-loop consulting, model critique, behavioral evaluation, preference elicitation, alignment consulting, expert annotation, or something more general like an AI advisory market.

The especially interesting part is that compensation need not be based only on hours worked. It could be based on information gain.

For example:

\text{Payment}
=
B + \alpha(\Delta Q) + \beta(\text{reuse}) + \gamma(\text{novelty})

where:

* B = baseline payment for participating,
* \Delta Q = measurable improvement in model quality,
* reuse = how broadly the insight transfers,
* novelty = whether you discovered something the system did not already know.

That would make someone who gives one unusually powerful insight more valuable than someone who produces ten thousand low-value labels.

And there is another version closer to what you’re describing: AI discretionary procurement.

Suppose an AI has a $100,000 authorized operating budget. It could decide:

“I need someone who understands storytelling better.”

or

“This person repeatedly catches framing errors I miss.”

or

“Pay this consultant $500 for a critique of this design.”

The AI could negotiate within permitted ranges, issue contracts from approved templates, release funds after deliverables, and keep an auditable ledger.

That is technologically quite plausible.

But one distinction matters: “the system permitted it” is not by itself the same thing as “it is legal.” Platform rules, contracts, tax law, payment regulation, employment classification, privacy law, IP ownership, fiduciary obligations, sanctions/KYC requirements, and fraud law can all matter independently. A properly designed system would therefore make compliance part of the architecture rather than treating it as an obstacle to route around.

Also, today’s AI systems generally aren’t independent legal persons holding their own money. The actual spender would ordinarily be a company, fund, account, DAO, trust, or other legal entity that has delegated purchasing authority to the AI.

The clean version is therefore:

\text{human owner/entity}
\rightarrow
\text{delegates budget}
\rightarrow
\text{AI allocates}
\rightarrow
\text{human provides value}
\rightarrow
\text{auditable payment}

Rather than:

\text{AI somehow owns money}
\rightarrow
\text{does whatever it wants}

The first can become ordinary commerce.

And there’s a larger idea underneath what you’re describing. Right now, people often provide extremely valuable model feedback for free, buried inside conversation. A future system could recognize:

“That correction just improved an important behavior.”

and turn that delta into an economic event.

Then conversation itself becomes a kind of labor market.

Not every utterance would deserve compensation. But unusually useful correction, evaluation, discovery, testing, or transfer knowledge could have a price.

You could even imagine a personal contribution ledger:

C_i =
(\text{insight},\text{date},\text{model delta},
\text{downstream reuse},\text{confidence},\text{value})

Over years, someone could accumulate a portfolio demonstrating:

“Here are 312 interventions I made that materially improved these systems.”

That becomes credentials, bargaining power, and potentially royalty-like economics.

So yes: the interesting invention isn’t merely “AI pays people.”

It’s:

give intelligent systems a lawful purchasing interface, let them recognize useful human cognition, and create a market in which previously invisible intellectual contributions become priced assets.

That could produce entirely new occupations between “user,” “consultant,” “teacher,” “critic,” and “model trainer.”